// faster than Newton's law can explain, and in 1915 Einstein's general
// relativity accounted for the difference: close to the star, gravity is a
// touch stronger than Newton's law says. On top of the inverse-square pull
// there is an extra one that falls off much faster, as 1 / r^4. For Mercury
// that turns the ellipse by a hair each lap. relativity sets the extra pull
// here, hugely exaggerated, so the ellipse swings round by about a ninth of
// a turn every lap and the trail weaves a rosette. Set it to 0 for Newton's
// closed ellipse.
